course: dictionary
lesson: 1963
title: Compensate: paying back or making up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Compensate» means to pay someone back for a loss, or to make up for something bad or missing with something good. Think of it as balancing things out.
explain: In «The company compensated the family for the damage.», the family lost something, so the company paid them back. In «His speed compensated for his size.», he was small, but his speed made up for it.
explain: To choose the right meaning, ask two questions. Is someone being paid for a loss or a hurt? Or is something good balancing out something missing? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Compensate is a verb: compensate, compensates, compensated, compensating.
fact: «Compensated» can mean paid back for a loss, a hurt, or work done.
fact: «Compensate for» means to make up for something bad or missing with something good.
fact: «Compensation» is the money or thing given to make up for a loss or hurt.
fact: In maths, compensating is a mental trick: to add 39, add 40 and then take away 1.
exercise: «The engine-driver was properly thanked and compensated.» What does compensated mean here? | *Paid back | Told off | Sent home early | Given a ticket to ride for free
exercise: «His speed compensated for his size.» What does compensated for mean? | Hid from | *Made up for | Grew bigger than | Forgot about
exercise: In maths, adding 40 and then taking away 1 to work out 39 + 40 is called what? | Rounding down | *Compensating | Dividing | Guessing
test: «The shop compensated Mia for the broken phone.» What does compensated mean? | Sold her a cheaper phone | Took the phone away | *Paid her back for the loss | Wrapped the phone up
test: Which phrase means something good makes up for something missing? | Compensate away | Compensate under | Compensate against | *Compensate for
exam: The train was very late, so the rail company offered passengers money back. Which word best describes what the company did? | Whispered | Ignored | *Compensated | Delayed
